{"type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Clone & Split\n      The single canvas visually \"clones\" into three copies. The blue and red hands separate from the original — blue slides down-left, red slides down-right — while simultaneously scaling to 50%. The hero slides up slightly. All three canvases keep animating throughout.\n      \n        Pros\n          Feels like pulling apart a mechanism\n          User sees the hands physically separate\n          Animation never pauses — seamless continuity\n        \n        Cons\n          Needs overlay/clone elements during transition\n          Most complex to implement (fake canvases during morph)","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1773269423876}
{"type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      Shrink & Reveal\n      The assembled canvas shrinks to hero size (slides up, scales to ~67% width). As it moves, two empty slots fade in below. Then the blue and red canvases fade/scale in from 0% to 100% in their slots. Two-phase: shrink hero, then reveal children.\n      \n        Pros\n          Simpler — no clone trickery needed\n          Clear two-step sequence is easy to follow\n          Works with existing AnimatorCanvas instances\n        \n        Cons\n          Brief moment where small slots are empty (less seamless)\n          Feels less \"mechanical\" — more like a UI transition","choice":"b","id":null,"timestamp":1773269425170}
{"type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Crossfade Morph\n      The assembled layout crossfades directly to the disassembled layout. Both exist simultaneously during the transition with opposing opacity. Simple, fast, clean — like a page turn.\n      \n        Pros\n          Simplest to implement\n          Fast — no waiting for multi-stage animation\n          Familiar pattern (modal/page transitions)\n        \n        Cons\n          Doesn't communicate the \"pulling apart\" concept\n          Feels generic — not delightful or educational\n          Misses the opportunity to show what's happening","choice":"c","id":null,"timestamp":1773269425698}
